.\" Man page for mdsh, the Make Diagnosis Shell.
.\" Render without installing anything:  man ./mdsh.1
.\" Plain text:  man ./mdsh.1 | col -bx > mdsh.txt
.\" Only standard man(7) macros are used: .TH .SH .SS .TP .PP .RS .RE .nf .fi
.\"
.TH MDSH 1 "2026-09-26" "mdsh" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
mdsh \- the Make Diagnosis Shell, a diagnostic wrapper around /bin/sh
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdsh
.RI [ shell-arguments ...]
.PP
.B mdsh
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.PP
.B mdsh
.BR \-H | \-\-HELP
.PP
.B make
.B SHELL=mdsh
.RI [ MDSH_ *=...]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdsh
is a shell wrapper designed for analyzing makefile behavior, though it
can be used outside of
.BR make .
Its main use case is diagnosing problems in complex, often massively
parallel, builds.
.PP
.B mdsh
execs the underlying shell
.RB ( /bin/sh
by default) passing its
.I argv
through unparsed and unexamined, so apart from the extra fork and exec
it behaves exactly like that shell. The single exception is that
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-\-help ,
.B \-H
and
.B \-\-HELP
print its own usage message instead of the shell's.
.PP
All of the added behavior is controlled by environment variables in the
.B MDSH_*
namespace, which leaves the option namespace free for the underlying
shell. Those variables trigger actions before or after the shell runs:
reporting file state changes, printing commands and timings, recording a
.SM CSV
database of every command, or dropping into an interactive debug shell.
.PP
Typically
.B mdsh
is selected by passing
.B SHELL=mdsh
to
.BR make ,
along with whichever
.B MDSH_*
variables are wanted. For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ MDSH_TIMING=1 make SHELL=mdsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
prints every command run by every recipe with its run time. Note that
SHELL must be overridden explicitly on the command line due to GNU make
semantics while MDSH_* options can be found from the environment.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.B mdsh
has no options of its own; everything on the command line belongs to the
shell. The following are the only arguments it interprets itself, and
only when they appear as the last argument.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-HELP
Print a usage message (including advanced and experimental options) and
exit.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.SS Watching files
.TP
.B MDSH_PATHS
A colon-separated list of glob patterns naming files to watch. After the
command finishes any change of state is reported. See
.B REPORTS
below.
.IP
A pattern containing no
.B /
is matched against the base name of every file below the current
directory, so
.B '*.o'
finds object files at any depth without needing
.BR '*/*.o' .
A pattern which does contain a
.B /
is expanded as an ordinary glob relative to the current directory.
Directories are reported only when created or removed, never when an
entry within them changes. The
.B .git
and
.B .svn
directories are never descended into.
.TP
.B MDSH_ABSPATH
If set (nonzero), report all
.B MDSH_PATHS
changes by absolute path. See
.B REPORTS
for what is reported otherwise.
.TP
.B MDSH_MARKER
An optional string added to report lines to aid later grepping. The
default marker is
.BR ==\-== .
.
.SS Printing commands
.TP
.B MDSH_XTRACE
If set, print each shell command to stderr much as
.B "set \-x"
would.
.TP
.B MDSH_TIMING
Like
.B MDSH_XTRACE
but the command is printed with its run time. Useful for profiling a
build to find the long poles. After running
.B make
with this enabled, plus
.B \-Orecurse
for recursive parallel builds, sort recipes by run time with something
like:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ grep MDSH_TIMING: sample.log | sort \-n \-k3,3
.fi
.RE
.IP
Timings are also recorded by
.BR MDSH_DB .
.TP
.B MDSH_PWD
If set, print the current working directory before each shell command.
.TP
.B MDSH_VERBOSE
If set (nonzero), print the working directory and the command line along
with each
.B MDSH_PATHS
report.
.TP
.B MDSH_NOFIXUP
By default
.B mdsh
tidies whitespace in commands before printing them, since make recipes
often expand to long, hard to read strings containing runs of spaces and
embedded newlines. Newlines and tabs become spaces, runs of spaces are
squeezed to one, and leading and trailing spaces are dropped. This flag
suppresses that. Note that the tidied form is for reading only; no
commitment is made that it can be fed back to a shell.
.
.SS Recording a database
.TP
.B MDSH_DB
If present, names a writable directory. Each shell command drops one
file there, named for its start time in nanoseconds and its pid,
summarizing the command in
.SM CSV
format. See
.B "CSV DATABASE"
below.
.
.SS Debugging interactively
.TP
.B MDSH_CMDRE
A regular expression compared against the shell command. On a match, an
interactive debug shell is invoked before the command runs.
.TP
.B MDSH_DBGSH
If set and the underlying shell exits with a failure status, run an
interactive shell to help analyze the failing state.
.IP
Both of the above can run into trouble under
.BR "make \-j" ,
which generally closes stdin. Interactive shells need stdin and stdout
connected to the terminal.
.TP
.B MDSH_SHELL
Override the default shell
.RB [ /bin/sh ]
which
.B mdsh
execs. This does not tell
.B make
which shell to use; that is what
.B SHELL=mdsh
does.
.
.SS Flushing NFS caches (experimental)
.TP
.BR MDSH_PRE_FLUSH_PATHS ", " MDSH_POST_FLUSH_PATHS
Colon-separated lists of paths on which to attempt NFS cache flushing
before or after the command runs. The first thing done with each listed
path, if it is a directory, is to open and close it, which may flush the
filehandle cache.
.TP
.B MDSH_HTTP_SERVER
The name of an HTTP server with read access to the listed files. A GET
request is issued for each path in
.BR MDSH_PRE_FLUSH_PATHS ,
file or directory, which is said to force dirty NFS caches for that path
to be flushed.
.IP
NFS cache flushing is a complex topic and the situation varies by
protocol version, server vendor and so on. Multiple techniques are
supported, in both a pull model (flush before reading) and a push model
(flush after writing), to allow experimental tuning. A linker recipe
might flush the directory holding its object files with
.BR MDSH_PRE_FLUSH_PATHS=$(@D) ,
or flush
.B $^
directly. Pull is generally thought more correct than push, but having a
compile recipe push-flush its target with
.B MDSH_POST_FLUSH_PATHS=$@
may be worth trying.
.\"
.\" Undocumented on purpose, pending further consideration:
.\"
.\" MDSH_BASEDIR   exported by the first mdsh to run and inherited by the
.\"                rest; see REPORTS. Can be set by hand to pin reporting
.\"                to a chosen directory but this is not yet advertised.
.\" MDSH_EFLAG     on command failure, send SIGINT to the parent process.
.\" MDSH_XTEVS     colon-separated list of environment variable names to
.\"                print along with each traced command.
.
.SH REPORTS
A state change reported through
.B MDSH_PATHS
looks like this:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdsh: [1] ==\-== CREATED: sub/foo.o
.fi
.RE
.PP
The fields are the program name, the value of
.B $(MAKELEVEL)
in square brackets if present, the marker, the state change
and the path. The level is present only when running under
.BR make ,
and is included because in a recursive build each
.B make
in the chain may report the same change. State changes are:
.TP
.B CREATED
The path did not exist before the command and does now.
.TP
.B REMOVED
The path existed before the command and does not now.
.TP
.B MODIFIED
The modification time advanced.
.TP
.B ACCESSED
The access time advanced.
.PP
Paths are reported relative to the directory of the first
.B mdsh
in the process tree so a file keeps the same name at every level of a
recursive build. Files outside that directory are reported by absolute
path as are all files when
.B MDSH_ABSPATH
is set.
.PP
On Linux, mount points normally carry the
.B relatime
option under which a read updates the access time only if it is not
already later than the modification time. To make reads visible,
.B mdsh
pushes the access time of each watched file back behind its modification
time before the command runs.
Systems that record every read, such as macOS, need none of
this and are left untouched.
.
.SH "CSV DATABASE"
If
.B MDSH_DB
is provided, each command writes one
.B .csv
file into that directory whose fields are:
.PP
.RS
.nf
START TIME,PID,PPID,STATUS,ELAPSED,USER TIME,SYS TIME,
LOAD AVG,MAKELEVEL,PWD,COMMAND
.fi
.RE
.PP
A
.B FORMAT.txt
file describing that format is written to the same directory.
.B LOAD AVG
is the one-minute system load average when the command finished or
.B \-
if it could not be determined.
.B MAKELEVEL
is
.B \-
when not running under
.BR make .
.B COMMAND
must be last because it has unbounded length and may contain unquoted commas.
Elapsed time covers the command itself; work done by
.B mdsh
on its behalf falls outside the measurement.
.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Like any shell,
.B mdsh
exits with the status of the command it ran. A command killed by a
signal yields 128 plus the signal number, as a shell would report it.
.PP
An internal error in
.B mdsh
is always fatal. Once the command has run its status is reported in
preference to a generic failure, so a failed command's own status
survives; if the command succeeded, the internal error is reported as
.B EXIT_FAILURE
instead.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run in sequence:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ MDSH_PATHS=foo:bar mdsh \-c 'touch foo'
mdsh: ==\-== CREATED: foo

$ MDSH_PATHS=foo:bar mdsh \-c 'touch foo bar'
mdsh: ==\-== MODIFIED: foo
mdsh: ==\-== CREATED: bar

$ MDSH_PATHS=foo:bar mdsh \-c 'grep blah foo bar'
mdsh: ==\-== ACCESSED: foo
mdsh: ==\-== ACCESSED: bar

$ MDSH_PATHS=foo:bar MDSH_VERBOSE=1 mdsh \-c 'rm \-f foo bar'
mdsh: ==\-== REMOVED: foo [/bin/sh \-c rm \-f foo bar]
mdsh: ==\-== REMOVED: bar [/bin/sh \-c rm \-f foo bar]

$ MDSH_TIMING=1 mdsh \-c 'sleep 2.4'
+ [MDSH_TIMING: 2.4s] mdsh \-c 'sleep 2.4'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch every object file in a tree, however deep:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ MDSH_PATHS='*.o' make \-j12 SHELL=mdsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Real-life usage via make:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ MDSH_PATHS=foobar MDSH_VERBOSE=1 make \-j12 SHELL=mdsh ...

$ make SHELL=mdsh MDSH_DBGSH=1 ...

$ rm \-rf /tmp/db; mkdir /tmp/db; MDSH_DB=/tmp/db make SHELL=mdsh ...
.fi
.RE
.
.SH "MAKE AND .ONESHELL"
.B make
keeps a compiled-in list of shells it knows to be POSIX-conformant.
Unfortunately
.B mdsh
is not on that list even though it wraps
.B /bin/sh
and thus effectively is a POSIX shell. Not knowing this can confuse
.BR make ,
particularly in
.B .ONESHELL
mode. The workaround is to invoke
.B mdsh
under a name that IS on the list. A symlink
.B "rksh \-> mdsh"
is probably the best choice, since
.B rksh
is recognized but almost nobody uses it as an actual shell; it may be
simplest to always use that name and avoid surprises.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B FORMAT.txt
Written into the
.B MDSH_DB
directory, holding the
.SM CSV
header line.
.TP
.B mdsh\-csv
Companion script which summarizes and sorts an
.B MDSH_DB
directory.
.
.SH NOTES
The usage message printed by
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-HELP
is generated from the source and is the most complete and up-to-date
documentation.
.PP
A basename pattern in
.B MDSH_PATHS
requires
.B mdsh
to walk the directory tree twice per command, once before and once after,
which may have some measurable time cost, but
.B mdsh
is intended for diagnostic use, not production builds. This overhead is
not included in timing measurements.
.
